---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import manifest from '../../../data/finger-atlas-comparisons.json';
const base = '/assets/finger-pathway-atlas/v3/';
const groups = [...new Set(manifest.comparisons.map(c => c.group))];
const sourceById = new Map(manifest.sources.map(s => [s.id, s]));
---

<Layout pageTitle="Finger atlas · Reference images" compactHeader wide subtitle="" description="50 comparison plates and 42 original scientific figures accompanying the Finger Pathway Atlas V3. Source, species, tissue and model limitations are retained for each comparison.">
  <header class="reference-heading"><p class="edition">Finger Pathway Atlas · V3 / V4 references</p><h1>Model and real tissue</h1><p>50 comparison plates · 42 original figures · 19 studies and the H01 gallery</p><nav aria-label="Atlas versions"><a href="/simulations/finger-pathway-atlas/v3/">← V3 original atlas</a> · <a href="/simulations/finger-pathway-atlas/v4/">V4 development atlas →</a></nav></header>
  <p class="reading-note">Left: the supplied V3 model, retained as the development baseline. Right: published scientific tissue images used to guide V4 morphology. These plates are not V4 screenshots. The two panels have independent magnifications and represent different specimens, fields and scales. Source species and imaging methods are identified on every plate.</p>
  <section class="sources cortical-methods" id="cortical-methods">
    <h2>M1, S1 and the six-layer cortical sections</h2>
    <p><a href="/simulations/finger-pathway-atlas/v4/?view=cortex">Open the paired coronal sections →</a>. Both gyri remain visible at a shared physical zoom. The body view displays only the left precentral and postcentral gyri. The paired inspection panels use separate anterior–posterior planes: they are modeled MRI-style tissue sections, not a patient scan or a single slice placing M1 and S1 next to each other.</p>
    <p>M1 uses a 3.4 mm cortical thickness prior, a thin layer IV and an expanded layer V; S1 uses a 2.2 mm prior and a more prominent granular IV. These numerical fractions, cell counts and placements are construction parameters. Layer VI follows the gray–white boundary and contains separate soma-connected corticothalamic projection examples. The M1 corticospinal examples originate in V. A continuation below the section means “axon continues,” not a measured synapse or a reconstructed thalamic endpoint.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adk4858" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shapson-Coe et al. (2024), A petavoxel fragment of human cerebral cortex reconstructed at nanoscale resolution.</a> Human temporal-cortex ultrastructure and laminar reference. <a href="https://h01-release.storage.googleapis.com/data.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Official H01 data and CC BY 4.0 license</a>; <a href="https://h01-release.storage.googleapis.com/layers.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">layer distributions</a>. H01 is not M1 or S1. V4 imports twelve connected proofread neurite trees, two per source layer, then makes separately identified morphology transplants in the two regions. It does not replicate H01’s complete volume or connectivity.</li>
      <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1111/ejn.12585" target="_blank" rel="noopener">García-Cabezas & Barbas (2014), Area 4 has layer IV in adult primates.</a> Rhesus histology supports a thin, sparsely populated M1 IV and prevents falsely removing that layer. The model’s human layer fractions are not measurements from these animals.</li>
      <li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8896626/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Location of the central sulcus via cortical thickness of the precentral and postcentral gyri on MR (1996).</a> MRI evidence for the relative thickness difference used to distinguish the gyri; not a source segmentation for these meshes.</li>
      <li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17395383/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thalamocortical and the dual pattern of corticothalamic projections of the posterior parietal cortex in macaque monkeys (2007).</a> Tracing evidence distinguishes layer-VI and layer-V corticothalamic projection patterns; used as a cross-area primate connectional reference, not direct proof of individual human M1/S1 endpoints.</li>
    </ol>
    <details open><summary>How DKI should guide the axon tracts</summary>
      <p>Use diffusion MRI to constrain the <strong>local orientation distribution of a bundle</strong>. Fit diffusion and kurtosis tensors to suitable multi-shell diffusion data after motion, distortion and gradient corrections. Estimate the kurtosis diffusion orientation distribution, retain credible directional peaks, and integrate trajectories with stated curvature, step-size and tissue-mask rules. Register the result to cortical gray–white surfaces in physical coordinates. The 3D rendering then sweeps a cylindrical surface along each modeled path; radius is a separate histological prior.</p>
      <p>V4 currently uses anatomical trajectory priors; it has not fitted DKI data. DKI does not identify single axons, their physical diameters, one-to-one cell attachments or synapses. Multiple paths must remain separate at crossings; streamlines must not merge into an artificial common neuron. Do not interpret a streamline count as an axon count. The displayed sparse fiber spacing is a rendering/sample choice, not a DKI measurement.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/mrm.20508" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jensen et al. (2005), Diffusional kurtosis imaging: The quantification of non-Gaussian water diffusion by means of magnetic resonance imaging.</a> Basis of the non-Gaussian diffusion expansion.</li>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/nbm.3053" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jensen, Helpern & Tabesh (2014), Leading non-Gaussian corrections for diffusion orientation distribution function.</a> Uses diffusion and kurtosis tensors to estimate orientations, including crossing bundles.</li>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/nbm.3374" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Glenn et al. (2015), Optimization of white matter fiber tractography with diffusional kurtosis imaging.</a> Reproducibility, efficient orientation estimation and handling of nonphysical negative ODF values.</li>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A4714" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Glenn et al. (2016), Mapping the Orientation of White Matter Fiber Bundles.</a> Comparison of DTI, DKI and DSI orientation estimates.</li>
        <li><a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2011.06.006" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fieremans, Jensen & Helpern (2011), White matter characterization with diffusional kurtosis imaging.</a> Compartment modeling under restricted assumptions, including approximately aligned bundles; not a general individual-axon diameter estimator.</li>
      </ol>
    </details>
    <p><a href="/assets/finger-pathway-atlas/v4/h01/reference-neurites.json" download>H01 source IDs, physical coordinates, radii and provenance</a>. The source SWC coordinates were verified against original H01 skeleton coordinates. Connected neurite topology is retained with a 0.7 µm centerline simplification tolerance. Cylindrical radius approximates the source skeleton, not the original EM membrane surface. The visible-fiber option widens display radius; switch it off to use physical diameter.</p>
  </section>
  <div class="filters" role="search" aria-label="Filter reference plates">
    <label>Region<select id="reference-group"><option value="">All regions</option>{groups.map(group => <option value={group}>{group.replace(/^\d+\s+/, '')}</option>)}</select></label>
    <label>Find a plate<input id="reference-query" type="search" placeholder="e.g. myelin, human, brainstem, EM" /></label>
    <span id="reference-count" role="status">50 plates</span>
  </div>
  <p id="reference-empty" hidden>No plates match. Choose All regions or clear the search.</p>
  <div class="plate-grid">
    {manifest.comparisons.map(c => {
      const source = sourceById.get(c.reference_source)!;
      return <article class="plate" id={`plate-${c.number}`} data-group={c.group} data-search={[c.title,c.species,c.tissue,c.modality,source.short,c.compare,c.gap].join(' ').toLowerCase()}>
        <button class="plate-image" type="button" data-full={base + c.plate_file} data-title={`${String(c.number).padStart(2,'0')} · ${c.title}`} aria-label={`Enlarge plate ${c.number}: ${c.title}`}><img src={base + c.thumbnail_file} alt={`${c.title}: V3 model beside ${c.species.toLowerCase()} ${c.modality.toLowerCase()} reference`} width="960" height="656" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></button>
        <div class="plate-content"><p class="plate-number">{String(c.number).padStart(2,'0')} · {c.group.replace(/^\d+\s+/, '')}</p><h2>{c.title}</h2><p class="metadata">{c.species} · {c.modality}<br />{c.tissue}</p><p>{c.compare}</p><details><summary>Model differences and attribution</summary><p>{c.gap}</p><p>{c.model_basis}</p><p>{c.scale_notice}</p><p><a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.short} — {c.panel}</a></p><p>{source.rights_notice || 'The source retains its copyright and reuse terms; consult the original publication.'}</p>{source.license_links?.map(url => <p><a href={url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source license</a></p>)}</details>
        <nav class="plate-links" aria-label={`Plate ${c.number} resources`}><a href={`/simulations/finger-pathway-atlas/v3/?view=${c.region}`}>Inspect in V3</a><a href={`/simulations/finger-pathway-atlas/v4/?view=${c.region}`}>Inspect in V4</a><a href={base + c.full_figure_file} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original figure</a><a href={base + c.plate_file} download>Full plate</a></nav></div>
      </article>;
    })}
  </div>
  <section class="sources" id="sources"><h2>Sources and provenance</h2><p>Original full figures and full-size supplied comparison plates are preserved. Gallery thumbnails are resized display copies. The comparison notes, crops, species, model basis and reference hashes remain in the <a href={base + 'comparison_manifest.json'} download>comparison manifest</a>; camera states and model-image provenance remain in the <a href={base + 'model_render_provenance.json'} download>render provenance</a>.</p><p><a href={base + 'SOURCES.md'} download>Download source bibliography</a></p>
    <ol>{manifest.sources.map(source => <li><a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.short}</a> — {source.title}{source.doi && <span> · DOI: {source.doi}</span>}</li>)}</ol>
  </section>
  <dialog id="plate-dialog" aria-labelledby="plate-dialog-title"><div class="dialog-top"><h2 id="plate-dialog-title"></h2><button id="plate-close" type="button">Close</button></div><img id="plate-full" alt="" /><a id="plate-download" download>Download full-size plate</a></dialog>
</Layout>

<script>
  const group = document.querySelector<HTMLSelectElement>('#reference-group')!;
  const query = document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('#reference-query')!;
  const plates = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.plate')];
  function filter() {
    const terms = query.value.toLowerCase().trim().split(/\s+/).filter(Boolean);
    let visible = 0;
    for (const plate of plates) {
      plate.hidden = Boolean(group.value && plate.dataset.group !== group.value) || !terms.every(term => plate.dataset.search?.includes(term));
      if (!plate.hidden) visible++;
    }
    document.querySelector('#reference-count')!.textContent = `${visible} ${visible === 1 ? 'plate' : 'plates'}`;
    document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#reference-empty')!.hidden = visible > 0;
  }
  group.addEventListener('change',filter); query.addEventListener('input',filter);
  const dialog = document.querySelector<HTMLDialogElement>('#plate-dialog')!;
  let opener: HTMLElement | null = null;
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('.plate-image').forEach(button => button.addEventListener('click', () => {
    opener = button;
    const image = document.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>('#plate-full')!;
    image.src = button.dataset.full!; image.alt = button.dataset.title!;
    document.querySelector('#plate-dialog-title')!.textContent = button.dataset.title!;
    document.querySelector<HTMLAnchorElement>('#plate-download')!.href = button.dataset.full!;
    dialog.showModal();
  }));
  document.querySelector('#plate-close')!.addEventListener('click', () => dialog.close());
  dialog.addEventListener('click', e => { if (e.target === dialog) { const r=dialog.getBoundingClientRect(); if(e.clientX<r.left||e.clientX>r.right||e.clientY<r.top||e.clientY>r.bottom)dialog.close(); } });
  dialog.addEventListener('close',()=>{document.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>('#plate-full')!.removeAttribute('src');opener?.focus();});
</script>

<style>
  .reference-heading{margin:28px 0 20px}.edition,.plate-number{font-size:11px;letter-spacing:1px;color:var(--muted);text-transform:uppercase}h1{font-size:clamp(36px,5vw,58px);font-weight:400;margin:8px 0}h2{font-size:25px;font-weight:400;line-height:1.2;margin:0 0 12px}p{font-size:14px;line-height:1.65}.reading-note{max-width:920px;border-left:2px solid var(--borderStrong);padding:8px 18px}
  .filters{display:flex;gap:20px;align-items:end;margin:26px 0}.filters label{display:grid;gap:7px;font-size:12px}.filters input,.filters select{font:inherit;font-size:14px;border:1px solid var(--borderStrong);border-radius:7px;padding:10px;background:var(--panel);color:var(--text);min-width:250px;max-width:100%}#reference-count{padding:10px 0;font-size:13px;color:var(--muted)}
  .plate-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr));gap:24px}.plate{border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:12px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--panel)}.plate[hidden]{display:none}.plate-image{display:block;padding:0;width:100%;border:0;cursor:zoom-in;background:#f3f2ef}.plate-image img{width:100%;height:auto;display:block}.plate-content{padding:20px}.plate-number{margin:0 0 10px;font-size:10px}.metadata{font-size:12px;color:var(--muted)}details{margin-top:15px}summary{font-size:13px;cursor:pointer}details p{font-size:12px;color:var(--muted)}.plate-links{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:16px;font-size:12px;border-top:1px solid var(--border);padding-top:15px;margin-top:18px}
  .sources{margin:40px 0;max-width:960px}.sources li{font-size:13px;line-height:1.7;margin:0 0 12px}.sources span{color:var(--muted)}
  dialog{padding:18px;max-width:min(1500px,96vw);max-height:94vh;overflow:auto;border:1px solid var(--borderStrong);border-radius:10px;background:var(--panel);color:var(--text)}dialog::backdrop{background:rgba(0,0,0,.8)}.dialog-top{display:flex;gap:20px;justify-content:space-between;align-items:start}.dialog-top h2{font-size:22px}.dialog-top button{font:inherit;background:var(--panelHover);color:var(--text);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 14px;cursor:pointer}#plate-full{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;margin:5px 0 15px}#plate-download{font-size:13px}button:focus-visible,input:focus-visible,select:focus-visible,summary:focus-visible{outline:2px solid currentColor;outline-offset:3px}
  @media(max-width:760px){.plate-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.filters{flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch;gap:12px}.filters input,.filters select{width:100%;min-width:0}.plate-content{padding:16px}#reference-count{padding:0}}
</style>
